-- examples/checks/async-reserved.rofl — A PROGRAM THAT MUST BE REFUSED.
--
-- START.md section 7 forbids shipping `@async` `as implemented instead of
-- explicitly rejected`, and section 1 reserves the syntax. A prohibition of that
-- shape has exactly one mechanical form: write the forbidden thing down and
-- require the loader to say no. Both spellings were probed and both are
-- refused -- the tense marker on a PREMISE and on a HEAD -- and the head form
-- is the one kept here, because it is the one a person reaching for async
-- would write.
--
-- The golden holds the refusal, so a kernel that quietly starts ACCEPTING this
-- is a red. That is the whole difference between a reserved word and an
-- unimplemented feature.

p(X) @async :- q(X).
